{"name":"Just Right","hex":"#dcbfac","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"just","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Factually right, correct; factual.","Rationally right, correct."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Only, simply, merely.","Introduces a disappointing or surprising outcome that renders futile something previously mentioned."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/just"},{"term":"right","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Designating the side of the body which is positioned to the east if one is facing north, the side on which the heart is not located in most humans. This arrow points to the reader's right: →","Clockwise, particularly when describing a change in direction or orientation."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["On the right side.","Towards the right side."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/right"}],"entries":[{"title":"Goldilocks and the Three Bears","blocks":[{"text":"\"Goldilocks and the Three Bears\" is a traditional classic 19th-century British fairy tale of which three versions exist. The original version of the tale tells of an impudent and bad old woman who enters the forest home of three anthropomorphic bachelor bears while they are away. She eats some of their porridge, sits down on one of their chairs, breaks it, and sleeps in one of their beds. When the bears return and discover her, they see her wake up and jump out of the window, and she is never seen again. The second version replaces the old woman with a young, naive, blonde-haired girl named Goldilocks; and the third and by far best-known version replaces the bachelor bears with a family of three: a father bear, a mother bear, and a baby bear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_Three_Bears_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17034.jpg/330px-The_Three_Bears_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17034.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldilocks_and_the_Three_Bears","wikidata":"Q128917"},{"title":"Just Right","blocks":[{"text":"Just Right is a breakfast cereal produced by Kellogg's. It was introduced in 1982 intended for health-conscious, athletic Australians, who are the world's third-biggest cereal eaters. The cereal is sold in Iceland, Ireland, New Zealand and Australia but has been discontinued in other markets, including the United Kingdom. A Canadian Just Right cereal, also manufactured by Kellogg's but with somewhat different ingredients, was available in Canada from 1985 to 2021."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Justright_brand_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just_Right","wikidata":"Q6316329"},{"title":"Just Right (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Just Right\" is a song recorded by South Korean boy group Got7 for their third extended play Just Right. It was released by JYP Entertainment on July 13, 2015."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just_Right_(song)","wikidata":"Q138542310"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_Three_Bears_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17034.jpg/500px-The_Three_Bears_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17034.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_Three_Bears_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17034.jpg/500px-The_Three_Bears_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17034.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Three_Bears_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_17034.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/The_story_of_the_three_bears_1839_pg_21.jpg/500px-The_story_of_the_three_bears_1839_pg_21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wiki